WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.318
Transfer is the ability to apply learning beyond the original practice context.

2
00:00:05.318 --> 00:00:11.966
Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles what the person has already practiced.

3
00:00:11.966 --> 00:00:16.398
Far transfer requires adapting principles to a meaningfully different situation.

4
00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:27.921
Expertise often transfers well when the underlying structure is similar, the cues are recognizable, and the expert understands which parts of the old model still apply.

5
00:00:27.921 --> 00:00:30.580
Expertise does not transfer perfectly everywhere.

6
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:31.910
Domain boundaries matter.

7
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:39.001
A strong performer in one technology, industry, organization, or operating environment may struggle when assumptions change.

8
00:00:39.001 --> 00:00:41.660
This is not a personal failure.

9
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:47.864
It is a reminder that expert judgment depends on the conditions that shaped it.

10
00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:54.955
When those conditions change, the expert needs fresh evidence, updated models, and sometimes deliberate re-training.

11
00:00:54.955 --> 00:01:00.274
Brittle expertise performs well under familiar conditions but struggles when novelty appears.

12
00:01:00.274 --> 00:01:07.365
It may depend on outdated assumptions, narrow examples, or a lack of exposure to edge cases.

13
00:01:07.365 --> 00:01:21.547
Expert blind spots can also emerge because deeply skilled people may forget what beginners do not know, overlook weak signals outside their usual pattern, or become overconfident when a situation feels familiar.

14
00:01:21.547 --> 00:01:24.206
Adaptable expertise requires humility and updating.

15
00:01:24.206 --> 00:01:32.626
Strong experts ask what has changed, which assumptions may no longer hold, and what evidence would change their mind.

16
00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:37.058
They seek feedback from the environment and from other people.

17
00:01:37.058 --> 00:01:42.377
Continuous learning keeps expertise alive because real domains do not stand still.

18
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:49.468
Tools, threats, markets, regulations, teams, and technologies all change the conditions under which judgment must operate.
